speechtext:
It imposes an obligation on the Secretary of State to give him notice wherever revocation of his certificate is asked for upon any of the grounds set forth in paragraphs (a), (c), or (e) of subsection 2. That is where he:
(a) Has, during any war in which His Majesty is engaged unlawfully traded or communicated with the enemy;
(c) was not of good character at the date of the grant of the certificate;
(c) remains, according to the law of a state at war with His Majesty, a subject of that state.
In all these cases the Secretary of State is required to give him notice. In the other cases the Secretary of State may, if he deems proper, hold an inquiry before making his report to the Governor in 'Council. In that respect this is entirely the provision as it was passed last year the only difference being the inclusion of subsection (b).
